Original per-file notices remain authoritative: BSD-2-Clause, except
fsutil.c (BSD-3-Clause). bx adaptation files are BSD-2-Clause.

The formatter and checker retain separate algorithms and process-owned state.
Their entry points are exec-only and their internal symbols are bx-prefixed.
The adapter shares retrying I/O with bx, bounds declared media sizes, checks
flush/close errors, and requires exclusive Linux block-device opens. Other
device backends return ENOTSUP; regular images use POSIX I/O.

newfs_msdos and fsck_msdosfs retain FreeBSD syntax. The mkfs.fat/fsck.fat
aliases use separate dosfstools-style option parsers, including EFI-oriented
FAT32 defaults. Unsupported options fail before opening the target. -C refuses
an existing path. Compatibility aliases follow symlinks without dropping the
exclusive block-device claim.

The layout calculation includes reserved FAT entries and computes the free
count from the final aligned geometry. The checker bounds data clusters by
FAT capacity and checks the standard FSInfo signature fields.
Geometry and media bounds are validated before FSInfo repair. FSInfo I/O
covers one filesystem sector. Non-mirrored FAT32 volumes use the selected
active FAT without updating inactive copies.

Relative paths are not rewritten beneath /dev. Disk-type database lookup is
unsupported. Formatting can set ASCII labels; standalone labeling and full
dosfstools repair-policy/output compatibility are not implemented.
